  "image_name": "a48ea1bd6ea3b662af32450369f79627.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic explains how magma stored in a crustal reservoir can either feed an eruption or stall, depending largely on viscosity, pressure, and dike size. A rounded magma chamber sits at roughly 4–10 km depth within strong wall rocks, with inward labels showing its radius, overpressure, and the tangential stresses and cracks it generates in the surrounding host rock. Magma enters from below at a given supply rate, inflating the reservoir and increasing pressure.\n\nThe key comparison is between two upward-propagating dikes: a larger dike can continue to the surface, whereas a smaller one appears unable to erupt because it cools and crystallizes before reaching shallow levels. The figure therefore links eruption potential to the ability of magma to fracture rock and maintain flow during ascent.\n\nText boxes summarize the physical controls. Magma viscosity is much lower for mafic magma than felsic magma, while the surrounding rocks are extremely viscous and elastic, with listed shear modulus, relaxation time, fracture toughness, and tensile strength. A small inset graph shows temperature decreasing with distance while log viscosity rises, reinforcing why narrow dikes freeze out more easily. Overall, the figure presents a mechanical model of reservoir pressurization and dike propagation.",
